    {
      "id": "stage-note",
      "type": "textlabel",
      "x": 620,
      "y": 230,
      "rot": 0,
      "label": "",
      "showLabel": false,
      "params": {
        "text": "The fundamental stands 200 mm off the axis and crosses it twice, so this arm carries\n2 × 200 mm = 400 mm of extra path: moving the stage by Δx moves the delay by 2Δx / c.",
        "fontSize": 10,
        "fill": "#34454d"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "delay-note",
      "type": "textlabel",
      "x": 620,
      "y": 590,
      "rot": 0,
      "label": "",
      "showLabel": false,
      "params": {
        "text": "The delay line matches it, ΔL = 400 mm = 1.33 ns, so the two pulses reach the crystal together.\nRetune it by 0.06 mm — 30 µm of stage travel — and the 200 fs pulses no longer overlap: the signal goes out.",
        "fontSize": 10,
        "fill": "#34454d"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "legend",
      "type": "textlabel",
      "x": 30,
      "y": 770,
      "rot": 0,
      "label": "",
      "showLabel": false,
      "params": {
        "text": "Both colours come from one laser, so the two trains are synchronous by construction: 515 nm and 1030 nm mix to 343.3 nm only where they coincide in time.\nThe crystal's Mixing readout gives the arrival difference and the overlap; scanning the delay through zero traces the cross-correlation.\nPhase matching, the polarizations the two beams would need, focusing overlap and depletion of either beam are not modelled: the signal is an authored fraction gated by arrival time.",
        "fontSize": 10,
        "fill": "#34454d"
      }
    }
